<div class="wrap prose">
  <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Architects to know</p>
  <h1>Famous architects every student should know</h1>
  <p class="lead muted">A starter canon of fifteen architects across the centuries &mdash; enough to follow most studio references and to know whose work to look up next.</p>

  <p>No list of fifteen can be complete or perfectly balanced, and the canon below leans, like most survey courses, on Western and modern figures &mdash; a bias worth noticing and reading against. Treat these as anchor points rather than a hierarchy. Each entry gives the single contribution that makes the architect matter, so that when a tutor drops a name in a crit you can place it. Once someone here sparks your interest, chase down a monograph and, better still, visit a building.</p>

  <h2>Fifteen to start with</h2>
  <p>Roughly chronological, from the Renaissance to the twenty-first century.</p>
  <table class="data">
    <tr><th>Architect</th><th>Era</th><th>Why they matter</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Andrea Palladio</td><td>1508&ndash;1580</td><td>Codified classical proportion into villas and treatises so influential they spawned &ldquo;Palladianism&rdquo; across Europe and America.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Christopher Wren</td><td>1632&ndash;1723</td><td>Rebuilt London after the Great Fire; St Paul&rsquo;s Cathedral remains a masterclass in dome engineering and English Baroque.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Antoni Gaud&iacute;</td><td>1852&ndash;1926</td><td>Fused nature, structure and craft into a singular organic language; the Sagrada Fam&iacute;lia is still rising in Barcelona.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Frank Lloyd Wright</td><td>1867&ndash;1959</td><td>Pioneered &ldquo;organic architecture&rdquo; and the open plan; Fallingwater fuses building and landscape like nothing before it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ludwig Mies van der Rohe</td><td>1886&ndash;1969</td><td>Distilled modernism to &ldquo;less is more&rdquo;: precise steel-and-glass buildings of extraordinary restraint, such as the Barcelona Pavilion.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Le Corbusier</td><td>1887&ndash;1965</td><td>Perhaps the most influential and divisive modernist; his &ldquo;five points&rdquo; and urban theories shaped a century of building.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Alvar Aalto</td><td>1898&ndash;1976</td><td>Brought warmth, timber and human sensitivity to modernism, proving the movement need not be cold or industrial.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Louis Kahn</td><td>1901&ndash;1974</td><td>Gave modern architecture monumental weight, mastery of natural light and a near-spiritual sense of material and order.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Oscar Niemeyer</td><td>1907&ndash;2012</td><td>Sculpted concrete into sweeping curves; his civic buildings gave the new capital of Bras&iacute;lia its soaring identity.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>I. M. Pei</td><td>1917&ndash;2019</td><td>A master of geometry and civic gesture; the Louvre Pyramid showed how bold modern form can transform a historic setting.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Norman Foster</td><td>b. 1935</td><td>Leading figure of high-tech architecture, marrying engineering, sustainability and refinement from the Gherkin to airports worldwide.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Renzo Piano</td><td>b. 1937</td><td>From the Centre Pompidou to The Shard, a lifelong pursuit of lightness, craft and structural clarity.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rem Koolhaas</td><td>b. 1944</td><td>Architect-theorist whose writing and OMA projects reframed how the profession thinks about the city and programme.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Zaha Hadid</td><td>1950&ndash;2016</td><td>Pushed fluid, parametric form further than anyone; the first woman to win the Pritzker Prize in her own right.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tadao Ando</td><td>b. 1941</td><td>Self-taught master of concrete, light and silence; his serene, minimal spaces show how little architecture needs to move you.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Widening the canon</h2>
  <p>Once you know these, deliberately look beyond them. Study Lina Bo Bardi and her radical civic spaces in Brazil, Denise Scott Brown&rsquo;s reshaping of urban theory, Balkrishna Doshi&rsquo;s humane modernism in India, Kazuyo Sejima&rsquo;s ethereal work with SANAA, and Francis K&eacute;r&eacute;&rsquo;s community-built architecture in Burkina Faso. A canon is a starting point; a good architect keeps expanding it.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> Do not just memorise names &mdash; for each architect here, learn one building well enough to sketch its plan from memory and explain the single idea it embodies. That depth is far more useful in a crit than a long list of half-remembered projects.</div>
